.TH SH-PLOT 1
.SH NAME
plot \- shell script interface to plot(2)'s graph command language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load plot

.B id := ${plot new
.I WxH
.RB [ title ]
.B }
.br
.B plot
.I id
.B cmd
.I graph-command...
.br
.B plot
.I id
.B draw
.br
.B ${plot
.I id
.B rows
.I name
.B }
.br
.B ${plot
.I id
.B value
.I name row column
.B }
.br
.B ${plot
.I id
.B invert
.I view x y
.B }
.br
.B plot del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Plot
is a loadable module for
.IR sh (1)
giving shell scripts direct access to
.IR plot (2)'s
own graph command language
.RB ( table / colour / view / content / split / axis / line / scale /
.B clear
and any table's own
.BR append ,
documented in full in
.IR plot (2)).
.IR Plot (2)
itself is display-agnostic \(em
.B Plotter.new()
just takes an image and a font, and draws into whatever image it's
handed \(em so the one thing this module adds beyond forwarding to
.BR Plotter.cmd()/rows()/value()/invert()
is a window to draw
.I into:
a handle here is a small
.B Toplevel
(opened the same way
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
opens its own \(em a
.B panel
widget inside its own frame, packed to fill; an offscreen buffer image
the same size, bound to it via
.BR tk->putimage ())
plus the
.B Plotter
itself, over that buffer. This is independent of
.IR sh-tk (1)'s
own window handles \(em that module's own window table is private to
it, unreachable from here.
.PP
Every verb this module does
.I not
list above
.RB ( table / colour / view / content / split / axis / line / scale /
.BR clear ,
and anything
.IR plot (2)
adds later, plus any table's own
.B append
verb) is
.IR plot (2)'s
own language, forwarded verbatim via
.BR "plot\ id\ cmd\ ..." \c
\&; this file never recognizes any of it, the same discipline
.IR sh-dynsys (1)
and
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
itself already hold to.
.PP
.B ${plot new
.I WxH
.RB [ title ]
.BR }
opens the window (a bare top-level
.B sh
gets a fresh draw context of its own; an
.B sh
already hosted inside one \(em under
.IR wm (1),
say \(em reuses it, the same ambiguity
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own
.B init()
resolves the identical way) and yields a handle.
.B Plot
.I id
.B cmd
.I graph-command...
joins the remaining words back into one line and hands it to the
handle's own
.BR Plotter.cmd() ,
raising an error if
.IR plot (2)
itself rejects it.
.B Plot
.I id
.B draw
calls the handle's own
.BR Plotter.draw() ,
flushes the buffer, and marks the panel dirty so the window actually
redraws \(em the same sequence
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own
.B redraw()
ends with.
.PP
.BR Rows ,
.BR value ,
and
.B invert
are
.IR plot (2)'s
own functions of the same name, unchanged.
.B Plot del
closes the window and frees the handle.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
	load plot
	id := ${plot new 400x300 History}
	plot $id cmd 'table history t y'
	plot $id cmd 'colour ink 16r4ec9b0ff'
	plot $id cmd 'content margin 10 10 10 10'
	plot $id cmd 'view v content'
	plot $id cmd 'scale v x 0 10'
	plot $id cmd 'scale v y 0 100'
	plot $id cmd 'history append 0.0 0.0'
	plot $id cmd 'history append 5.0 25.0'
	plot $id cmd 'history append 10.0 100.0'
	plot $id cmd 'axis v x time'
	plot $id cmd 'axis v y value'
	plot $id cmd 'line v history x t y y colour ink width 2'
	plot $id draw
	echo rows: ${plot $id rows history}
	plot del $id
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/plot.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR plot (2),
.IR tk (2),
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-tk (1),
.IR sh-dynsys (1),
.IR sh-pde (1),
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
.SH BUGS
.B Plot
.I id
.B cmd
.B table
must not be reissued after any
.B append
\(em
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.B bind()
unconditionally replaces a table with a fresh, empty one, silently
discarding every appended row (see
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own BUGS-adjacent header comment for the incident this was first found
from). This module does nothing to protect a script from that; timing
which commands run once versus every redraw is the caller's own
business, the same as it is there.
